    "title_en": "Detection of 2 MeV Spectral Bump and ∼40 MeV Emission Line from GRB 221009A",
    "abstract_en": "Gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) are the most energetic transients in the Universe, but prompt MeV-sub-GeV observations remain challenging with current gamma-ray observatories. Here, using unsaturated HEPP-H measurements on board CSES-01 and cross calibrating with likewise unsaturated GECAM-C data, we reconstruct the continuous spectral evolution of GRB 221009A over 2-255 MeV. We identify an unusual spectral bump at ∼2 MeV in the first light-curve peak. We also confirm a narrow emission line whose energy decreases from ∼40 MeV following a t$^{−1}$ evolution, and whose flux shows a \"rise-then-fall\" profile, implying weak emission from the central jet. These findings hint that a magnetically dominated, central-empty jet exists in the brightest of all time GRB.",
